#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial reproduces the error-detected, approximate preparation of a W "
"state by using dynamic circuits, as presented in [Smith, Pokharel, Maurya, "
"Takita, \"Resilience Beyond the Light Cone: Error-Detected Primitives for "
"Practical Dynamic Circuits\" (2026)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) "
"[\\[1\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The approach is an adaptation of the algorithm presented in [Piroli, "
"Styliaris, Cirac, \"Approximating many-body quantum states with quantum "
"circuits and measurements\" (2024)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07604) "
"[\\[2\\]](#references), upgraded with the *Distributed Control Error "
"Detection* (DisCo ED) scheme of [Smith et "
"al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.08925) The paper runs the protocol on a "
"heavy-hex processor. Here, the protocol runs on the square lattice of a "
"Nighthawk processor. The noisy simulation reproduces its main finding: both "
"DisCo ED checks improve the prepared state compared with no error detection,"
" and the implicit check gives the best state. On hardware at $n = 10$, the "
"idle time during the feedforward limits how much error detection can "
"recover, as discussed after the hardware results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following sections describe the ingredients of this tutorial in "
"detail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The W state {#the-w-state}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The $n$-qubit W state is the equal superposition of all states with exactly "
"one excitation,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|W_n\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\Big(|10\\cdots0\\rangle + |01\\cdots0\\rangle + \\cdots + |00\\cdots1\\rangle\\Big) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}|e_j\\rangle,\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $|e_j\\rangle$ has a single $|1\\rangle$ on qubit $j$. Unlike the GHZ "
"state, its entanglement survives the loss of any one qubit. This tutorial "
"characterizes the prepared state with two simple observables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Hamming-weight distribution.** Measure every qubit in the $Z$ basis and "
"count the number of 1s, $w$. A perfect W state gives $P(w=1) = 1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Coherence.** $P(w=1)=1$ alone does not certify a W state: an incoherent "
"mixture $\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_j |e_j\\rangle\\langle e_j|$, or a superposition "
"$\\sum_j \\pm|e_j\\rangle$ with random signs, also has $P(w=1)=1$. To probe "
"the relative phases, use\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"C = \\frac{n}{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\\sum_{i\\neq j}\\big\\langle X_iX_j + Y_iY_j\\big\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator $X_iX_j + Y_iY_j = 2(\\sigma_i^+\\sigma_j^- + "
"\\sigma_i^-\\sigma_j^+)$ moves an excitation between qubits $i$ and $j$. For"
" a state $\\sum_j c_j|e_j\\rangle$ it gives $\\langle X_iX_j + "
"Y_iY_j\\rangle = 4\\,\\mathrm{Re}(c_i^*c_j)$, so $C = 1$ for $|W_n\\rangle$,"
" $C = 0$ for the incoherent mixture, and random signs push $C$ toward zero. "
"Measuring $C$ takes only two extra circuits: one with every W qubit measured"
" in the $X$ basis, and one in the $Y$ basis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamic circuits {#dynamic-circuits}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A unitary circuit of nearest-neighbor gates spreads correlations only within"
" a *light cone* that grows by a fixed distance per layer. Preparing a long-"
"range entangled state such as a GHZ or W state on a line of $n$ qubits "
"therefore takes a depth that grows with $n$. Dynamic circuits, which use "
"mid-circuit measurements and classical feedforward, get around this limit: "
"measurement outcomes are processed classically and sent to qubits anywhere "
"on the chip, in a time that does not depend on distance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A paradigmatic example is the constant-depth GHZ state (see the [long-range "
"entanglement tutorial](/tutorials/long-range-entanglement) and "
"[\\[3\\]](#references)). The protocol splits the line into small *patches* "
"and prepares small GHZ states on each patch in parallel. It then *fuses* "
"neighboring patches by measuring the parity $Z_aZ_b$ across each boundary. "
"Outcome $m_k = 1$ means the two patches disagree. Applying $X$ to every "
"qubit of patch $k$ whenever the cumulative parity $m_1 \\oplus \\cdots "
"\\oplus m_k$ is 1 fixes every disagreement in a single round of feedforward."
" In this circuit, each measured qubit is then reset and re-entangled with "
"its own patch, so all $n$ qubits end up in\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\mathrm{GHZ}\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\big(|0\\cdots0\\rangle + |1\\cdots1\\rangle\\big),\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "in a depth that does not depend on $n$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Approximate preparation of the W state {#approximate-preparation-of-the-w-"
"state}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The idea of the W-state-preparation protocol of Ref. [\\[2\\]](#references) "
"is to start from a product state that overlaps strongly with $|W\\rangle$, "
"then *project* away the unwanted components. Rotating every qubit with "
"$R_Y(\\theta)$, where $\\sin^2(\\theta/2) = p$, gives\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"|\\phi\\rangle = \\bigotimes_{j}\\Big(\\sqrt{1-p}\\,|0\\rangle + \\sqrt{p}\\,|1\\rangle\\Big) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\sqrt{P_k}\\,|D_k\\rangle, \\qquad P_k = \\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"where $|D_k\\rangle$ is the uniform superposition of all weight-$k$ states "
"(the Dicke state, with $|D_1\\rangle = |W\\rangle$). For small $p$, almost "
"all of the weight is in $k=0$ and $k=1$. Projecting onto odd parity with "
"$\\Pi_{\\rm odd} = (1 - Z^{\\otimes n})/2$ removes $k = 0, 2, 4, \\ldots$ "
"and leaves\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\Pi_{\\rm odd}|\\phi\\rangle \\propto \\sqrt{P_1}\\,|W\\rangle + \\sqrt{P_3}\\,|D_3\\rangle + \\cdots .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The projection succeeds with probability $r_s = \\sum_{k\\,\\rm odd}P_k = "
"\\big[1 - (1-2p)^n\\big]/2$, and the fidelity with the W state is $F = "
"P_1/r_s$. Choosing $p = \\delta/n$, for large $n$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"F \\to \\frac{\\delta}{\\sinh\\delta} \\approx 1 - \\frac{\\delta^2}{6}, \\qquad r_s \\to \\frac{1 - e^{-2\\delta}}{2}.\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Neither depends on $n$, so the cost of the protocol does not grow with "
"system size. The parameter $\\delta$ trades fidelity against success rate. "
"This tutorial uses $\\delta = 0.3$, which gives $F \\approx 0.985$ and $r_s "
"\\approx 0.23$. The small remaining infidelity comes from higher odd-weight "
"components.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Single-ancilla projection.** One way to apply $\\Pi_{\\rm odd}$ is a "
"Hadamard test. Prepare an ancilla in $|+\\rangle$, apply $CZ$ between the "
"ancilla and every system qubit (a controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$), and measure "
"the ancilla in the $X$ basis:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$$\n"
"\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\Big(|0\\rangle|\\phi\\rangle + |1\\rangle Z^{\\otimes n}|\\phi\\rangle\\Big) = |+\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 + Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle + |-\\rangle\\,\\frac{1 - Z^{\\otimes n}}{2}|\\phi\\rangle .\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Outcome \"$-$\" heralds the odd sector. The catch is that a single ancilla "
"must interact with all $n$ qubits, requiring a circuit depth proportional to"
" $n$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Distributed control.** Replace the single ancilla with an $n$-qubit GHZ "
"state prepared via dynamic circuits, and place the GHZ qubit $g_j$ next to W"
" qubit $w_j$. The GHZ state behaves as one *logical* qubit, with $|\\bar "
"0\\rangle = |0\\cdots0\\rangle$, $|\\bar 1\\rangle = |1\\cdots1\\rangle$, "
"and logical operator $\\bar X = X^{\\otimes n}$. The controlled-$Z^{\\otimes"
" n}$ becomes $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ on every pair at once, a single layer of gates. "
"Measuring $\\bar X$ means measuring every GHZ qubit in the $X$ basis and "
"multiplying the outcomes, so an odd number of 1s heralds success. Every step"
" now has constant depth. On a square lattice, the GHZ line and the W line "
"form a *ladder*: two rows of $n$ qubits joined by $n$ rungs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Ladder layout of the GHZ line and the W line on the square "
"lattice](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-state/w-state-ladder.avif) "
"*(a) The GHZ line and the W line occupy two neighboring rows of the square "
"lattice and are joined by $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ rungs. (b) The GHZ line alone. The "
"staggered prep patches (shaded) are fused by mid-circuit measurements at the"
" marked boundaries. Each boundary falls inside one of the DisCo ED detection"
" pairs (check $c$, root $r$), so a faulty fusion is detected.*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "DisCo ED {#disco-ed}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"While the distributed control technique reduces the procedure to a constant "
"depth circuit, it does not come for free.  It requires $n$ ancillas and, "
"more importantly, $\\lceil n/2 \\rceil - 1$ mid-circuit measurements. "
"Readout error is typically slightly worse than two-qubit gate error on "
"superconducting devices, so implementing this strategy can introduce "
"significant noise. Additional challenges include the fact that the GHZ "
"qubits sit idle during the mid-circuit measurements and feedforward, making "
"them susceptible to relaxation and dephasing errors, and the final $\\bar X$"
" readout requires measurement of an $n$-qubit operator.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To dig further into the impact of noise, consider the effect of each type of"
" error on the GHZ state throughout this protocol:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Bit flips:** The CZ layer turns $X_{g_j}$ (with $g_j$ denoting the $j$-th "
"GHZ qubit) into $X_{g_j}Z_{w_j}$ (with $w_j$ denoting the $j$-th W state "
"qubit), so a bit flip on $g_j$ applies $Z$ to $w_j$. Because $X_{g_j}$ "
"commutes with $\\bar X$, the herald is unaffected, and the error flips the "
"sign of one W amplitude. The Hamming weight is blind to this, but $C$ "
"drops.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Fusion readout errors:** Because the parity of fusion mid-circuit "
"measurement outcomes determines the pattern of X-corrections applied via "
"feedforward when preparing the GHZ state, a wrong fusion outcome creates a "
"pattern of bit flip errors called a *domain wall*. Depending on how the "
"feedforward corrections are applied, a domain wall between sites $j$ and "
"$j+1$ produces a bit-flip error on all GHZ qubits to the left (or right) of "
"the domain wall. The impact of these bit flip errors on the state is as "
"discussed above.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Phase flips and readout errors** on the GHZ qubits flip the herald, so the"
" protocol accepts the even-parity sector by mistake. These show up as weight"
" 0 and weight 2 in the Hamming-weight distribution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"DisCo ED starts from the observation that the GHZ state is a repetition "
"code: its stabilizers are $Z_jZ_{j+1}$ and its logical operator is $\\bar "
"X$. A plain readout measures each qubit in the $X$ basis, which reveals "
"$\\bar X$ but by gathering independent pieces of information that are not "
"useful to the protocol (that is, each individual $X$). The idea of DisCo ED "
"is to replace these \"useless\" measurements with more useful ones, while "
"still gathering the necessary information to construct $\\bar X$. In "
"particular, group the GHZ qubits into pairs $(2k, 2k+1)$, each with a "
"*check* qubit $c = 2k$ and a *root* qubit $r = 2k+1$. Because $X_cX_r$ and "
"$Z_cZ_r$ commute, you can measure both. A $CX$ from $r$ to $c$ maps $X_cX_r "
"\\to X_r$ and $Z_cZ_r \\to Z_c$, so afterward:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the root, measured in the $X$ basis, gives bit $r_k$, the value of $X_cX_r$."
" The product of all root outcomes is $\\bar X$, so the herald is an odd "
"number of 1s among the $r_k$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the check, measured in the $Z$ basis, gives bit $f_k$, the value of the "
"stabilizer $Z_cZ_r$. Without errors, $f_k = 0$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This gives two variants.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Explicit check:** $CX(r\\to c)$, then $H$ on $r$. Reject the shot if any "
"$f_k = 1$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Implicit check:** add a second $CX(r\\to c)$ after the $H$. The root still"
" gives $X_cX_r$, but the check now measures the product $X_cX_r \\cdot "
"Z_cZ_r$, so without errors $f_k = r_k$. Reject the shot if any $f_k \\ne "
"r_k$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both variants check the same stabilizers. The difference is what happens "
"when a readout error occurs on a root qubit: it flips the herald without "
"touching any stabilizer, so the explicit check misses it, while the implicit"
" check catches it because $f_k$ no longer matches $r_k$. The implicit "
"readout $CX\\cdot H\\cdot CX$ compiles to the same single two-qubit gate as "
"$CX\\cdot H$, so this extra protection costs no additional two-qubit "
"gates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each pair is a detection patch of size $d = 2$. The paper also considers "
"larger patches, which check more stabilizers at the cost of a deeper "
"readout; this tutorial uses $d = 2$ throughout. With $d=2$, DisCo ED "
"detects:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**any single bit flip on a GHZ qubit**, because every GHZ qubit belongs to "
"exactly one checked pair.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**fusion errors**, provided the domain wall falls *inside* a pair. This is "
"why the GHZ preparation, as shown below, uses the staggered patches $[0], "
"[1,2], [3,4], \\ldots$: their boundaries $(0|1), (2|3), \\ldots$ lie inside "
"the detection pairs $[0,1], [2,3], \\ldots$. If the prep patches were "
"aligned with the pairs, every domain wall would fall *between* two pairs, "
"where nothing is checked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**readout errors on the root qubits**, with the implicit check only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"DisCo ED does not detect phase flips on the GHZ qubits, or any error on the "
"W qubits themselves, so postselection improves the state without making it "
"perfect. The price is the fraction of shots discarded (the *acceptance rate*"
" is less than 1) and one extra layer of two-qubit gates in the readout. No "
"additional qubits are needed over the non-error detected implementation.\n"
msgstr ""

