#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setup {#setup}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The cell below imports the required tools and defines the helper functions "
"used to analyze the results. The functions that build the circuit are "
"defined step by step in the small-scale example below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Each circuit stores its measurement outcomes in up to four classical "
"registers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`fusion`: the parity measurements used to fuse the GHZ patches\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`ghz`: the GHZ readout bits that herald the W state (the root of each pair, "
"or every GHZ qubit with no error detection)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`flag`: the DisCo ED check bits (one per pair; not present without error "
"detection)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`w`: the final measurement of the W qubits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The analysis has three steps. `postselect` keeps only the shots that pass "
"the DisCo ED check (`flag` is cross-checked with `ghz`) and that herald the "
"odd-parity sector (an odd number of 1s in `ghz`). The kept `w` bits then "
"give the Hamming-weight distribution, the coherence $C$, and bounds on the "
"fidelity $F$ with $|W_n\\rangle$. Finally, `exact_reference` gives the ideal"
" values derived in the Background section. (If the circuits were built with "
"`feedforward=False`, `postselect` first applies the GHZ corrections that "
"were left for postprocessing. See Step 1 below.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, run the protocol in simulation for $n = 6$ W qubits (12 qubits in "
"total), in two ways:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Expected reference:** the ideal values derived in the Background section, "
"computed by `exact_reference`. `StatevectorSampler` cannot run circuits that"
" contain classical feedforward (`if_test`), so this example also runs the "
"logical (untranspiled) circuits on the noiseless `AerSimulator`. This checks"
" both the circuits and the postprocessing: without noise, every shot should "
"pass the DisCo ED check, and the results should agree with the expected "
"values up to shot noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Noisy run:** [local testing mode](/guides/local-testing-mode) with "
"`FakeMiami`, a fake Nighthawk backend with the same square lattice as the "
"hardware example. This shows how far a hardware-like result drifts from the "
"expected one, and how much of the gap DisCo ED recovers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-"
"inputs-to-a-quantum-problem}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The circuit uses two lines of $n$ qubits: the GHZ line `q_ghz`, which serves"
" as the distributed control, and the W line `q_w`, which ends up in the W "
"state. The circuit has three pieces, one for each stage of the protocol.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**1. Prepare the GHZ state.** `prepare_ghz` splits the GHZ line into the "
"staggered patches $[0], [1, 2], [3, 4], \\ldots$ (the last patch also takes "
"the final qubit) and prepares a small GHZ state in each patch. It then fuses"
" neighboring patches. A CNOT copies the parity $Z_aZ_b$ of the two qubits on"
" either side of a boundary onto $b$, the first qubit of the right patch. "
"Qubit $b$ is then measured into `fusion`, reset, and re-entangled with its "
"patch. Finally, the feedforward applies $X$ to every qubit of patch $k$ if "
"the parity of the first $k$ fusion outcomes is odd. The conditions are built"
" with `expr.bit_xor`, so all corrections happen in one round of "
"feedforward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Every correction is an $X$, and the rest of the circuit only moves it "
"around: the CZ layer turns $X_{g_j}$ into $X_{g_j}Z_{w_j}$, and the GHZ "
"readout carries it onto known bits. So the measurement statistics are the "
"same if, instead of applying the corrections on the device, you apply them "
"to the measured bits afterward (`deferred_corrections` and `postselect` in "
"the Setup cell). This is called tracking the *Pauli frame*. Note that it "
"does not prepare the W state on the device; it only reproduces the outcomes "
"that the device would have measured. `prepare_ghz` therefore applies the "
"corrections in real time by default (`feedforward=True`). Setting "
"`feedforward=False` is a useful diagnostic: it removes the wait for the "
"fusion outcomes to reach the controller and come back, during which the "
"whole GHZ line idles, so comparing the two settings shows how much error "
"that wait adds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**2. Seed and couple the W qubits.** `couple_w_qubits` rotates every W qubit"
" with $R_Y(\\theta)$, where $\\theta = 2\\arcsin\\sqrt{\\delta/n}$ so that "
"$p = \\delta/n$. It then applies $CZ(g_j, w_j)$ on every rung of the ladder,"
" which is the controlled-$Z^{\\otimes n}$ of the distributed Hadamard test. "
"The circuit applies the rotations after the GHZ preparation, just before the"
" CZ layer. That way, the W qubits wait in $|0\\rangle$, which does not "
"decay, during the slow mid-circuit measurements and feedforward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**3. Read out the GHZ state with DisCo ED.** `readout_ghz` measures the "
"logical $\\bar X$ of the GHZ state. With `check=None`, every GHZ qubit is "
"measured in the $X$ basis into `ghz`. With `\"explicit\"` or `\"implicit\"`,"
" the GHZ line is split into the pairs $(2k, 2k+1)$, as described in the "
"Background section. The root $r = 2k+1$ is measured into bit $k$ of `ghz`, "
"and the check $c = 2k$ into bit $k$ of `flag`. The only difference between "
"the two checks is the second CNOT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`build_w_state_circuit` puts the three pieces together, then measures the W "
"qubits in the requested basis. The barriers only separate the stages in the "
"circuit drawing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This example builds nine circuits for $n = 6$: three DisCo ED modes, each "
"measured in three bases. The $Z$-basis circuits give the Hamming-weight "
"distribution, and the $X$- and $Y$-basis circuits give the coherence $C$. "
"The cell below draws the implicit-check circuit with $Z$-basis measurements."
" From left to right, you can see the GHZ preparation, with its two fusion "
"measurements and the feedforward corrections (`if_test` blocks); the "
"rotations and the CZ rungs; and the paired DisCo ED readout. Note that the "
"two-qubit depth of each stage does not depend on $n$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-"
"state/extracted-outputs/b27b5511-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-"
"problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Transpile the circuits for `FakeMiami`. The snapshot behind this fake "
"backend does not list the `reset` and `if_else` instructions that real "
"Nighthawk processors support, so the code below adds them to its target "
"first. The simulator then models reset as noiseless.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The target qubit layout is a ladder: the GHZ line is a path of $n$ connected"
" qubits, and each W qubit is a neighbor of its GHZ partner, joined by a CZ "
"rung. Many ladders fit on the square lattice, and qubit quality varies "
"across the device, so `choose_layout` picks one from the backend's "
"calibration data. It estimates the expected number of errors of a ladder: "
"the CZ errors of the edges it uses, the readout errors of every measurement,"
" and the dephasing of the GHZ line while the fusion qubits are measured. It "
"grows candidate GHZ lines one qubit at a time, keeping the cheapest, then "
"gives each line its best W qubits. The code passes this layout to the "
"transpiler for all nine circuits, so that every mode runs on the same "
"physical qubits and the comparison between modes is fair. No SWAP gates are "
"needed, and the CZ depth is 4 without error detection and 5 with it. The "
"extra layer is the readout CNOT. The second CNOT of the implicit check "
"merges with the first one into a single CZ, so the implicit and explicit "
"circuits have the same CZ count.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-"
"primitives}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute the expected reference with `exact_reference`, then run the circuits"
" with `SamplerV2` in two ways: the logical circuits on a noiseless "
"`AerSimulator`, and the transpiled circuits on `FakeMiami`, with its noise "
"model. Each `(check, basis)` circuit is one PUB. Store the results in a "
"dictionary with the same keys as `circuits`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each mode, the table reports:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "the **acceptance**: the fraction of shots that pass the DisCo ED check\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"the **success**: the fraction of accepted shots that herald the odd-parity "
"sector, which should match the projection success rate $r_s$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**P(w=1)** and **C**, computed from the shots that are both accepted and "
"heralded\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**F**: a lower and an upper bound on the fidelity with $|W_n\\rangle$, "
"computed from the Hamming-weight distribution and $C$ (see "
"`fidelity_bounds`). The paper reports this fidelity, so it is the number to "
"compare with Ref. [\\[1\\]](#references).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The expected value of $C$ is slightly above 1. This is because the small "
"weight-3 component of the approximate W state has $C = 3(n-3)/(n-1) > 1$ "
"(see the comment in `exact_reference`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First, the noiseless run. Every shot passes the DisCo ED check (acceptance ="
" 1), and all three modes agree with the expected reference up to shot noise."
" Only about 900 of the 4,000 shots per circuit are heralded, so $C$ carries "
"a statistical uncertainty of about $\\pm 0.03$, and deviations of a few "
"hundredths are expected. So the error detection never discards a good shot, "
"and the circuits and postprocessing are correct.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Now the noisy run. The plot shows the Hamming-weight distribution of the "
"postselected W state (left), and the two observables $P(w=1)$ and $C$ "
"(right), compared with the expected values (gray).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-"
"state/extracted-outputs/cc659e30-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hardware runs {#hardware-runs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Steps 1–3: Map, optimize, and submit {#steps-13-map-optimize-and-submit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, put these steps together at a larger scale and submit the job to real "
"quantum hardware. Prepare a W state on $n = 10$ qubits, using 20 qubits of "
"`ibm_chicago`, a Nighthawk processor. The functions are the same as in the "
"small-scale example; only $n$, $\\delta$, the backend, and the number of "
"shots change. Because the protocol has constant depth, the transpiled "
"circuits have the same CZ depth as for $n = 6$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Three settings matter on hardware:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Layout.** `choose_layout` picks the 20 qubits from the current "
"calibration, and all nine circuits run on them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Fast readout and dynamical decoupling.** `fast_readout` swaps in the "
"device's faster measure and measure-and-reset instructions, and the GHZ line"
" runs a dynamical decoupling sequence while the fusion qubits are "
"measured.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**A larger $\\delta$.** On hardware, some even-parity shots pass the herald "
"(see the DisCo ED section). Most of them have weight 0, and their size "
"relative to the weight-1 component scales as $1/\\delta$. A larger $\\delta$"
" dilutes them, at the cost of more weight-3 contamination (about "
"$\\delta^2/6$). This tutorial uses $\\delta = 0.6$, which gives an ideal "
"fidelity of 0.95 and $r_s \\approx 0.36$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Only a fraction of the shots are kept, so this run uses 20,000 shots per "
"circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-"
"state/extracted-outputs/d58164ca-3b20-441c-9777-728497580cab-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format "
"{#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format-1}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The analysis is the same as for the small-scale example. Postselect each "
"mode, print the table, and compare the Hamming-weight distribution and the "
"two observables with the expected values for $n = 10$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/tutorials/error-detected-w-"
"state/extracted-outputs/f2a1c9b4-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"On hardware, DisCo ED recovers a clear part of the gap to the noiseless "
"result. Without error detection, $P(w=1)$ is about 0.67 and $C$ about 0.71. "
"With either check, $P(w=1)$ rises to about 0.76 and $C$ to about 0.87, and "
"the lower fidelity bound rises from about 0.50 to about 0.68, at the cost of"
" discarding about a third of the shots. The explicit and implicit checks "
"perform about the same in this run.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The remaining gap comes mostly from errors that DisCo ED cannot see. While "
"the fusion outcomes travel to the control electronics and back, the whole "
"GHZ line idles, and the phase errors it picks up flip the herald. DisCo ED "
"checks only the $ZZ$ stabilizers, so it does not detect these phase flips "
"(see the DisCo ED section above), and the success rate stays above $r_s "
"\\approx 0.36$. Two ways to explore this further:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Rebuild the circuits with `feedforward=False`. This removes the wait, so "
"comparing the two settings shows how much of the error the feedforward adds."
" The W state is then not prepared on the device, but without errors the "
"measured statistics are the same.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Try a larger $n$. More GHZ qubits idle during the wait, so the undetected "
"phase errors grow with $n$.\n"
msgstr ""

